    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, there can be internal balancing disruptions even if the external conditions seem right. Starter health is influenced by factors including hydration levels, feeding frequency, and the purity of ingredients. For example, contamination by unwanted bacteria or yeasts from handling or airborne drifts, even small amounts of starchy ingredients, or improper sealing of the jars can tip the balance. Salt, at the right levels, helps maintain the microbial balance but too much can suppress fermentation.\n\nImplementing strict sanitation practices, using filtered water, and ensuring airtight environments for your starters can help minimize the risks of contamination. Visualizing the population through a microscope or a syringe can also help you to anticipate or identify imbalances in your starter. Are there specific areas you're looking to perfect in your fermentation practices?",
